# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the GPU tests and the FFmpeg tests (used by the address-sanitizer
# preset only). A leak: entry suppresses a report when any frame of the allocation's stack is inside
# the named module.
#
# The Vulkan loader and what it loads (here Mesa lavapipe and the validation layer) are not
# instrumented. On the Linux CI runners a process that created a Vulkan instance sometimes ends
# with one 128-byte direct leak, allocated with realloc by a module the loader had loaded and
# already unloaded at exit (its frames show as "<unknown module>"), called through
# libvulkan.so.1. It comes and goes with no change to the code, the packages or the runner image,
# so it is the driver's or the layer's, not rawreel's. Because the allocating module is unloaded,
# only the loader's own frames can be matched.
#
# What stays checked: every allocation whose stack does not pass through the loader (all of
# rawreel's and the dvv libraries' own memory). What is hidden: memory allocated inside a Vulkan
# call, which includes a Vulkan object rawreel would fail to destroy; the lifetime registry's leak
# check (DVV_LEAK_CHECK=fail, set for the same tests) still reports the dvv objects that own them.
leak:libvulkan.so.1
